        ":question/problem": "Select all homogeneous second-order linear differential equations.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{\u2033}+b(x){y}^{\u2032}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{\u2033}+(x-2){y}^{\u2032}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+1){y}^{\u2033}-4x{y}^{\u2032}+3y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)\\,y+9=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{\u2033}+b{y}^{\u2032}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)\\,y=-9.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-9,$ which is different from zero."
      },
